  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 251 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nAnother short wave will arrive Friday night into Saturday as the \nmoisture plume continues to slowly track eastward resulting in \nanother round of widespread precipitation. More instability is \npossible over western Iowa on Saturday afternoon which could result \nin more convective activity but again, the threat for strong storms \nis low. Dryer air will arrive from west to east late Saturday night \ninto Sunday though mostly cloudy skies are expected to persist. \nMonday will be mostly dry and slightly warmer with highs in the 60s \nto near 70. Another strong short wave will arrive Tuesday and \nTuesday night. PWAT values will approach 2 inches and conditions \nwill be favorable for rainfall amounts of 1-2 inches. Note, Des \nMoines is currently a little over 2 inches from the September record \nfor precipitation. This will be approached with the system this \nweekend and the one early to mid next week. \n\nFor Wed into the end of the week, models are taking turns trying to \ningest the Pacific Hurricane Palo into southwest flow as a strong \nupper level low approaches the Four Corners region. There remains a \nlot of uncertainty here regarding this ingestion and the overall \nevolution of the upper level low but a slower progression of \neverything may lead to more wet weather for Iowa.",
